             comment = "Professor & Academic Mentor, Department of Statistics"))
Description: Design-based analysis of population health survey data from the
    World Health Organization (WHO) Stepwise Approach to Non-Communicable
    Disease (NCD) Risk Factor Surveillance (STEPS), Demographic and Health
    Surveys (DHS), Multiple Indicator Cluster Surveys (MICS) and similar
    complex sample surveys, where chronic conditions are self-reported rather
    than coded with the International Classification of Diseases (ICD).
    Provides survey design constructors for DHS, MICS and STEPS microdata;
    design-based prevalence for any binary indicators and groupings; a
    self-reported multimorbidity count based on the Functional Comorbidity
    Index (FCI) of Groll et al. (2005) <doi:10.1016/j.jclinepi.2004.10.018>,
    with condition-pattern analysis, sensitivity bounds for missing answers,
    and direct age standardisation to the WHO World Standard Population;
    standard, generalized, Erreygers and Wagstaff concentration indices with
    design-based standard errors following Kakwani, Wagstaff and van
    Doorslaer (1997) <doi:10.1016/S0304-4076(96)01807-6> and O'Donnell et al.
    (2016) <doi:10.1177/1536867X1601600112>, concentration curves, and slope
    and relative indices of inequality; categorisation of DHS-coded child
    growth z-scores; publication-ready tables and plots. Simulated example
    data with a known population are included. Experimental helpers for
    choropleth maps and case-weighted gradient boosting with SHapley Additive
    exPlanations (SHAP) are also provided.
